+## #4179 - The field simulator segments a move past the per-move ceiling, not its callers
+
+**Decided.** `SdfField::subtract_batch_fitted` is `subtract_batch` with every chord or arc piece whose travel passes `MAX_CELLS_PER_MOVE` cut as the fewest equal pieces that each pass it. The count is found by doubling from 2 until the pieces pass, then by bisection below that count. A chord is split at equal fractions of its ends (`chord_sweep` on each part); an arc piece is split into equal angular parts on the same circle with Z shared out uniformly (`ArcPiece::split`). Pieces shorter than one cell shrink no window further, so past that count it refuses by name. The four field arms of `Stock` (`simulate_field_with`, `simulate_undercut`, and the lathe and display arms) cut through it. `subtract_batch` and `subtract` keep the refusal as the bound of one update.
+
+**Why.** No caller segmented, so through `Stock` the field engine refused ordinary moves that the column engine cuts: a 12.7 mm endmill's straight 300 mm pass, a 127 mm flat tool's 6 mm plunge, an undercut disc's 6 mm plunge. The ceiling is reached by footprint times travel, so a caller's fixed piece length cannot be right for every tool. Splitting where the work is measured uses the same window count that the refusal reads, and covers every arm at once. A tool swept along the parts of a straight chord, or of an arc on one circle, sweeps the same solid as along the whole, so the cut is the same as an uncapped update. The work of a piece falls as it shortens, so doubling reaches a passing count; bisection relies on that fall being monotone, and where it is not, the count used still passes (correct, possibly not the fewest).
+
+**Considered and dropped.**
+- Splitting `Move`s in each caller, as the issue first proposed: a move carries an arc, a pose and a C angle, and each would need its own interpolation; the chord and arc piece are already the units the ceiling measures.
+- Removing the refusal from `subtract_batch`: one update's bound is the field's own memory and time law (#4079), and the issue asked to keep it.
+- A count from the footprint formula (the window's growth per millimetre of travel): it predicts the window, while the search measures the clipped window itself.
+
+**Cost to change later.** One public method, two private helpers in `field_cut.rs`, `ArcPiece::split` and `travel` in `field_arc.rs`, and one call in each of the four arms. Going back is calling `subtract_batch` in the arms. Pins: `crates/sim/tests/field_wide_tool.rs` (`an_endmills_300_mm_pass_cuts_on_both_engines`, `a_127_mm_tools_6_mm_plunge_cuts_on_both_engines`, `an_undercut_discs_6_mm_plunge_cuts_through_simulate_undercut`, and `a_wide_tools_long_chord_still_refuses_and_its_segments_cut` on `subtract_batch` directly).

